  ],
  "text": "There is almost no electricity stored in the grid. Generation matches demand in real time, always. You picture a reservoir somewhere with power sitting in it. There is not one. Switch a kettle on and something, somewhere, burns a little more fuel within seconds. And there is one dial that tells you whether the match is holding. Frequency. Too much demand and the generators are dragged slower. Too little and they run fast. Which is why operators forecast things you would never think were their problem. The end of a popular broadcast is a real event. Everyone gets up and switches on at once. Operators spin up reserve minutes before, because there is nothing in reserve otherwise. And when the balance breaks, equipment disconnects to protect itself, which unbalances the rest. That is a blackout. Not a shortage of power. A failure to match it, second by second. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
